  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 216 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\n***** STORM CHANCES WANE, HEAT BUILDS INTO THE WEEKEND *****\n\nThe effective boundary is forecast to drop southward into Kentucky \non Friday, possibly (30-50% chance) serving as a focus for \nadditional pop up storms during the afternoon mainly east of the I-\n65 corridor. However, the trend should be for ridging to expand \neastward heading into the weekend, suggesting chances for showers \nand storms will taper to less than 20% across the local area \nSaturday into Sunday. Meanwhile, conditions will grow increasingly \nhot and humid, with HeatRisk climbing solidly into the Major and \npossibly (20-40% chance) into the Extreme Category by Sunday. We \nremain close enough to the periphery of the ridge that if an MCS \ntakes a dive southward, it could (10-20% chance) wreck the \ntemperature forecast both Saturday and Sunday, but if that doesn't \nhappen (80-90% chance) then heat indices are almost certain to \nexceed 100 degrees across most/all of the area. We suspect NBM's \nforecast high temperatures might be a degree or two overdone given \nevapotranspiration (ET) after recent rain is sometimes underforecast \nby its inputs (global ensembles). However, the oppressive humidity \nwill combine with the heat to make conditions dangerous to \nvulnerable populations such as the elderly, chronically ill, outdoor \nworkers, and those without proper cooling. In addition, it will \nlikely prevent overnight low temperatures from falling below the mid \n70s (upper 70s in urban corridors).\n\n***** LESS HOT, STORMS RETURN EARLY NEXT WORK WEEK *****\n\nA shortwave trough should flatten the ridge and drop a frontal \nboundary over the area sometime early next work week, potentially \noffering some relief from the heat for at least a portion of the \narea and serving as a trigger for showers and storms. It's too early \nto tell whether a severe weather risk may materialize with this \nactivity, but the joint probability of > 25 kt 0-6km bulk shear and \n> 2000 J/kg MUCAPE from the LREF is less than 20%, which, at least \nat this point in time, suggests the risk should be relatively low. \nGiven the richly moist and unstable airmass ahead of the trough and \nattendant front, heavy rain and localized microbursts in water-laden \ncollapsing cores would seem to be the primary hazards (if any).",
